Plan: Per-course 4-digit certificate number
Context
Courses issue a certificate of completion. Today the certificate has no real number — the frontend shows a hardcoded serial 4987 on every certificate (CertificateLayout.tsx:24).

The client wants a unique 4-digit number per certificate, numbered per course starting at 0001 and increasing as new learners get their certificate. The number must be surfaced in the GET /api/progress/course/:courseId response (and in the certificate payload so it prints on the certificate).

Decisions confirmed with the user:

Stable per user — the number is assigned once the first time a learner opens/downloads their certificate for a course, stored, and reused on every re-download. The per-course counter only advances for new learners.
Per-course scope — each course has its own sequence (0001, 0002, …), independent of other courses.
Overflow — zero-pad to at least 4 digits, but allow growth beyond 9999 (10000…). Never collide, never break.
Why assignment happens at certificate-fetch time (not on the PNG "Download" click)
The actual "Download" button in Certificate.tsx is a pure client-side html-to-image render — the server never sees it. The server does see the data fetch GET /certificates/course/:courseId, which fires when the certificate opens and already gates on "course completed + has access + certificate enabled". Since the number is assign-once / stable per user, minting it there is correct: first open = number minted; every later open returns the same stored number. Same number is also echoed into progress/course.

Approach
Introduce a dedicated course_certificates table keyed by (user_id, course_id) that stores the assigned number. Assign lazily + atomically inside the existing certificate-data path, then echo the formatted number into both the certificate payload and the progress payload.

The legacy WP migration already sketches a certificates table (scripts\migrate-wp-to-ms.js:245-259) but nothing live uses it. We add a new, clean table via the repo's idempotent "ensure on boot" mechanism rather than reusing that dormant DDL, to avoid coupling to the one-time migration script.

1. New table + boot ensure (course-service)
Add SQL services/course-service/scripts/create-course-certificates-table.sql:
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS course_certificates (
  id            BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  user_id       BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL,
  course_id     BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL,
  cert_seq      INT UNSIGNED NOT NULL,          -- per-course running number (1,2,3,…)
  issued_at     DATETIME NOT NULL,
  created_at    DATETIME NOT NULL,
  updated_at    DATETIME NOT NULL,
  UNIQUE KEY uq_cert_user_course (user_id, course_id),
  UNIQUE KEY uq_cert_course_seq  (course_id, cert_seq)
);
cert_seq is the raw integer; the display string (0001) is derived at read time with String(cert_seq).padStart(4, '0').
Add services/course-service/src/db/ensureCourseCertificatesTable.js mirroring ensureCoursePageSettingsTable.js (read the .sql, run it once per process, set an ensured flag).
Wire it into connect() in models/index.js next to the existing ensure* calls.
Add a Sequelize model models/CourseCertificate.js (table course_certificates, underscored, timestamps: true with created_at/updated_at) and register it in models/index.js.
2. Atomic assignment helper (course-service)
Add getOrAssignCertificateNumber(userId, courseId) — new function in certificate.service.js (or a small certificateNumber.service.js). Logic:

SELECT cert_seq FROM course_certificates WHERE user_id=? AND course_id=? → if found, return it.
Else assign the next sequence atomically inside a transaction to avoid two concurrent first-time downloads getting the same number:
INSERT INTO course_certificates (user_id, course_id, cert_seq, …) VALUES (?, ?, (SELECT next), …) computed as COALESCE(MAX(cert_seq),0)+1 over the same course_id, guarded by the uq_cert_course_seq unique key.
On duplicate-key race (ER_DUP_ENTRY), retry the read/compute once (small bounded retry loop).
The uq_cert_user_course key guarantees one row per learner even under concurrent first opens.
Return cert_seq; caller formats via padStart(4,'0').
Reuse the existing sequelize from models; follow the raw-query style already in certificate.service.js / progress.service.js.

3. Surface the number in the certificate payload
In certificate.service.js:

getCertificateData and getCertificateDataInternal: after the existing completion/access/enabled checks pass, call getOrAssignCertificateNumber(userId, cid) and add certificateNumber: '0001' (formatted) to the returned object alongside courseTitle, studentName, completionDate.
Because assignment is gated behind the same checks, a number is only ever minted for a genuinely completed + certificate-enabled course — no wasted numbers.
4. Surface the number in progress/course
In progress.service.js getCourseProgress, after computing status:

Only when status === 'completed' and the course provides a certificate, look up (do not assign) the existing number: SELECT cert_seq … WHERE user_id, course_id. Return certificateNumber: cert_seq ? padStart : null.
Do not assign here — progress is polled frequently and also runs for internal summaries; minting on a background poll would burn numbers before the learner ever opens the certificate. Assignment stays exclusively in the certificate path (step 3). progress/course reports the number once it exists, null until then.
Add certificateNumber to the two return objects (the !batch early return can keep it null).
5. Frontend — display the real number
API types / payloads (study-cafe-web):
Add certificateNumber?: string | null to CertificatePayload (certificatePrint.ts:3), to the CertificateData type in Certificate.tsx:13, and to CourseProgressPayload (progress.service.ts:28).
CertificateLayout (CertificateLayout.tsx):
Add a serial?: string prop; replace hardcoded 4987 at line 24 with {serial ?? "—"}.
Pass it from Certificate.tsx render: serial={certificateData.certificateNumber} and from the print template (certificatePrint.ts) add a "Certificate Serial No." line using payload.certificateNumber.
